en by the corresponding bracket  to qualitatively understand the  heisenberg uncertainty principle we can  use a pictorial representation  of these expansions let's draw a set of  axes  and we place the energy eigenvalues on  the horizontal axis  and the expansion coefficient squared on  the vertical axis  we can then draw the representation of  this expansion like this  with the height of these lines  indicating the contribution of each  energy eigenstate to the expansion of  psi  in the energy basis  so how can we understand this in this  particular example the distribution pigs  around this value  so the eigenstate in this region make a  big contribution to the expansion of psi  and the eigenstates away from this peak  make a very small contribution to psi  the root mean square deviation of h then  simply measures the width  of this distribution this means that  small  values of delta h indicate that only a  few states are present in the expansion  of psi  whereas a larger delta h would indicate  that many states contribute  if we now look at the heisemberg  uncertainty principle up here  then it's giving us a relation between  the spread of the distributions when we  write the state psi  in the two bases associated with h and  with a  in particular if a and h do not commute  then the product of the two root mean  square deviations has to be larger than  some positive lower bounds  this means that if we draw the  corresponding pictorial representation  associated with observable a  then as in our example the delta h is  rather small  then the delta a must be rather large  and this is to ensure that their product  obeys the uncertainty principle this  means that the distribution must look  something like this  with a rather large delta a  just to be absolutely clear these two  distributions are a pictorial  representation of the same  state psi up here they're simply showing  the same state  in two different bases and what the  heisenberg uncertainty principle gives  us is a relation  between these two distributions  now this was a quick summary but you  should be familiar with this if you've  seen our videos on the heisenberg  uncertainty principle  if you haven't then i would encourage  you to check them out before you  continue  because our discussion today will build  on this and the heisenberg uncertainty  principle  isn't always explained very well so it  is important to make sure that we have a  clear understanding  before we proceed what we'll see today  is that the time  energy uncertainty relation is quite  different to this general uncertainty  relation  but at the same time we'll see it's not  completely unrelated to it  the next step requires a result that we  obtained in the video on the air infest  theorem  we can write the time derivative of the  expectation value of a general  observable q  as 1 over i h-bar times the expectation  value of the commutator of  q with the hamiltonian plus the  expectation value of the time derivative  of q in our case  we're working with an observable a and  in fact we can simply write it down as  equal to the first term  because in our setup in the first slide  we discussed that we're working with  observables that do not explicitly  depend on time  we can now isolate the expectation value  of the commutator  which is equal to ihbar multiplied by  the time derivative of the expectation  value  the next step is to use this latest  expression for the expectation value of  the commutator  and insert it into the uncertainty  principle up here  doing so we get that delta a delta h  is larger than or equal to this  we can simplify the right hand side to h  bar over 2  times the absolute value of the time  derivative  and finally we can rearrange the  expression to end up with the term  featuring the observable a  then delta h or larger than or equal to  one half times h bar  this term here has dimensions of time so  we can define a new quantity delta t  as equal to it to conform to 