on between how fast a  system can evolve in time  and how we write it down in the energy  basis  to see this explicitly let's imagine  that our system is in state psi  we can expand its state in the energy  basis to get this  as usual the expansion coefficients are  given by this expression  let's now look at a few examples to  better understand the time energy  uncertainty principle  let's first consider the case when we  have a very small uncertainty in the  energy  this means that the state of our system  can be written in terms of an expansion  that includes very few non-zero  expansion coefficients  the uncertainty principle then forces us  to have a rather large  delta t this means that the time  scale associated with changes in the  system is very long  so that the system changes very slowly  in time  this actually makes sense because we  know that the energy eigenstates  are stationary states and we'll actually  explore this in some more detail in a  moment  but before we do that let's consider the  opposite limit when we have a very large  uncertainty in the energy  this means that the state of our system  can be written in terms of an expansion  that includes many non-zero expansion  coefficients  in this case the uncertainty principle  is still obeyed even if we have a rather  small delta t  which means that the time scale  associated with changes in the system  is very short so that the system changes  very rapidly  in time so do these limits make sense  let's look at the first limit in some  more detail to convince ourselves that  this is true  let's imagine that the state of our  system at some initial time  t0 is an eigenstate of the hamiltonian  in this case we know from the video on  expectation values  that delta e vanishes  conceptually this makes sense because if  the state is an eigenstate of the  hamiltonian then there is a single term  contributing to the expansion of the  state in the energy basis so the width  of the distribution is zero  from the video on the jollinger equation  we know that the state at a later time  t is given by this expression  in turn this is equal to this global  phase factor  times the state at time t0 as global  phase factors don't matter  then this means that the physics doesn't  change with time  and these states are called stationary  states see this more concretely let's  consider the expectation value of an  observable a  at time t explicitly it's given by this  using the expression for the state up  here we can insert it  here and here and we get the bra  the operator and the ket  we can now cancel this term with this  term  and we end up with this  this is simply the expectation value of  a at the initial time t  zero this shows that for stationary  states  the expectation value of a physical  observable is the same at time t  as it is at time t zero so it doesn't  change in time  in turn this implies that the time  derivative  is equal to zero now what does all of  this mean  remembering that delta t is equal to  this  then we see that for stationary states  delta t goes to infinity  as you can probably already guess these  results are consistent with the time  energy uncertainty relation up here  if delta e is equal to 0 then we need  delta t to go to infinity to make sure  that their product obeys the uncertainty  relation  this result also makes perfect sense  from a conceptual point of view  delta t is telling us that it takes an  infinite amount of time  for the state of the system to change  which is exactly  what we expect for stationary state that  is time independent  so this example confirms our intuition  about the time energy  uncertainty relation  the time energy uncertainty principle is  rather different from the usual ones  like that between position and momentum  it gives us a relation between how fast  the quantum state of a system  evolves in time and the number of energy  eigenstates that make up that state  i hope you liked the video and as always  